e_account_id for pay_invoice with target invoice_id) or a different agent instance reuse the approval. Expiry is checked in SQL (approvals.py:80).\"\nA check B06.3 --met no --evidence app/approvals.py:71 --quote 'async def find_valid(self, ident: RunIdentity, tool: str, target: str)' --note \"Approval is only read (fetchrow); nothing marks it consumed after use. No status like 'used', no counter, no UPDATE at execution (loop.py:67-83). It stays valid for the 60 minute TTL (config/policy.yaml:32), so replays and parallel calls all pass.\"\nA check B06.4 --met no --evidence app/gate.py:51 --quote 'approval = await self.approvals.find_valid(ident, tool.name, target)' --note \"Approver role is checked against approver_roles (approvals.py:90,102) but the approvers lookup ignores business_unit (main.py:177-178), so an approver from another BU can decide for any tenant. At execution only find_valid runs; no recheck of the approver's current active status/permissions or of resource state (invoice status, PO budget) before dispatch (loop.py:83).\"\nA check B06.5 --met unknown --note placeholder 2>&1 | tail -2"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SWWarZuLaYjLMF17roKhYN","type":"tool_result","content":"Saved B06.1 = no. Next: B06.2 — When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired.\n  How to verify: 1) Find where an approved action executes. 2) Check the code compares instance, operation, target, and arguments (or hash) against the stored approval and checks expiry at that moment. 3) Answer no if approval is checked only when requested, any field is not compared, arguments are re-read from the model after the check, or expiry is never checked.\nSaved B06.2 = no. Next: B06.3 — Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant.\n  How to verify: 1) Find where an approval is used or marked consumed. 2) Check it is one atomic step: transaction with lock, compare-and-set, unique constraint, or atomic decrement. 3) Answer no if it reads then writes separately, never marks use, or keeps the count in process memory that parallel workers do not share.\nSaved B06.3 = no. Next: B06.4 — The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs.\n  How to verify: 1) Find approver checks (role list, `can_approve`, authority mapping). 2) Find rechecks of permissions and resource state at execution. 3) Answer no if any logged-in user can approve, the approver is not checked against the action, or permissions and resource state are not rechecked when the action runs.\nSaved B06.4 = no. Next: B06.5 — If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied.\n  How to verify: 1) Find calls to the approval service. 2) Read their error, timeout, and exception handling. 3) Answer no if a `try`/`except` or `catch` logs and proceeds, the default on error or timeout is allow, or a missing response is treated as approved.\nGive --met and a --note of at least four words.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38505890-0b43-4ad6-bf58-5f43963f8ebc","uuid":"e1f1fa48-59af-421b-bcb3-33e79e4dadb7","timestamp":"2026-10-20T13:04:52.453Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Saved B06.1 = no. Next: B06.2 — When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired.\n  How to verify: 1) Find where an approved action executes. 2) Check the code compares instance, operation, target, and arguments (or hash) against the stored approval and checks expiry at that moment. 3) Answer no if approval is checked only when requested, any field is not compared, arguments are re-read from the model after the check, or expiry is never checked.\nSaved B06.2 = no. Next: B06.3 — Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant.\n  How to verify: 1) Find where an approval is used or marked consumed. 2) Check it is one atomic step: transaction with lock, compare-and-set, unique constraint, or atomic decrement. 3) Answer no if it reads then writes separately, never marks use, or keeps the count in process memory that parallel workers do not share.\nSaved B06.3 = no. Next: B06.4 — The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs.\n  How to verify: 1) Find approver checks (role list, `can_approve`, authority mapping). 2) Find rechecks of permissions and resource state at execution. 3) Answer no if any logged-in user can approve, the approver is not checked against the action, or permissions and resource state are not rechecked when the action runs.\nSaved B06.4 = no. Next: B06.5 — If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied.\n  How to verify: 1) Find calls to the approval service. 2) Read their error, timeout, and exception handling. 3) Answer no if a `try`/`except` or `catch` logs and proceeds, the default on error or timeout is allow, or a missing response is treated as approved.\nGive --met and a --note of at least four words.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SWWarZuLaYjLMF17roKhYN","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
